		{"id":"13cb8a01471c3f5c","type":"text","text":"Keywords:\n* Hard/sharp vs smooth/organic\n* Isotropic vs anisotropic\n* Uniform sized cells vs global cell size variation\n* Regular vs irregular\n* Periodic vs non-periodic\n* Planar (2D) vs space (3D) tessellation","x":1844,"y":94,"width":532,"height":266},
		{"id":"6488a3461c0ff806","type":"text","text":"Categorization","x":1985,"y":-140,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"6f69b99cc5275fd0","type":"text","text":"A tessellation, or tiling, is a **division** of the plane (space) into figures (cells) called tiles using certain rules and constraints.\n\t\t\t\t\tVS\nA tessellation or tiling is the **covering** of a surface, often a plane, using one or more geometric shapes, called tiles, using certain rules and constraints.","x":620,"y":-600,"width":560,"height":245},
